<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lothar von Trotha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lothar von Trotha was a German general who commanded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a> during the war against the Herero and Nama. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterberg">Battle of Waterberg</a> in 1904 he issued an extermination order against the Herero, driving them into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omaheke">Omaheke</a> desert, where many died of thirst. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> formally acknowledged the killings of the Herero and Nama as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> in 2021.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lothar-von-trotha.jpg" alt="Lothar von Trotha" width="311" height="600" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lothar_von_Trotha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adrian Dietrich Lothar von Trotha was born on 3 July 1848 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> into a prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> noble family. He joined the Prussian Army in 1865, fought in the Austro-Prussian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian Wars</a> and was awarded the Iron Cross 2nd Class. In 1894 he was appointed commander of the colonial forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>, where he suppressed uprisings including the Wahehe Rebellion. He was later brigade commander of the East Asian Expedition Corps, which took part in suppressing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boxer-rebellion">Boxer Rebellion</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 3 May 1904 Trotha was appointed Commander in Chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> and directed to crush the Herero rebellion. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterberg">Battle of Waterberg</a> he had the Herero encircled on three sides, leaving only the waterless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omaheke">Omaheke</a> steppe as an escape. They fled into the desert, and he ordered his troops to poison water holes, to post guards along a line of 240 km and to shoot on sight any Herero trying to escape. His extermination order, the Vernichtungsbefehl, said that he would spare neither women nor children.</p><p>Public outcry led Chancellor Bernhard von Bülow to ask Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> to relieve him, but the survivors had by then been herded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> and used as labour, and many died. About 80,000 Herero were estimated to have lived there before the rising, and the 1911 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 15,000.</p><p>On 22 April 1905 Trotha told the Nama that those who did not surrender would be shot until all were exterminated. About 10,000 Nama died during the fighting, and the remaining 9,000 were confined to camps. He received the Pour le Mérite on 2 November 1905, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and retired the next year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Trotha died of typhoid fever in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> on 31 March 1920. In 2006 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> city council renamed a street named for him Herero Straße, and a street in Otjiwarongo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, was renamed Ohamakari in 2024. In October 2007 relatives of Trotha went to Omaruru on the invitation of Herero chiefs and publicly apologised. On 16 August 2004 the German government apologised for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> but rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, and in May 2021 it officially recognised the genocide and agreed to pay €1.1 billion over 30 years to fund projects in the affected communities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lothar von Trotha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Herero rising of 1904 and the Nama war under Hendrik Witbooi were crushed by General <b>Lothar von Trotha</b>; tens of thousands died in the desert and in concentration camps such as Shark Island.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>After the battle of Waterberg, General <b>von Trotha</b> ordered their extermination; most Herero died in the Omaheke desert or in camps.</blockquote><a href="/state/herero-paramountcy">Herero Paramountcy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.13,11.64&amp;year=1894" aria-label="Magdeburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4812 363 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lothar von Trotha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5262 633h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5262 633h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.13,11.64&amp;year=1894">What was here in 1894? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
